By cases: \begin{itemize} \item for $X$, the set of elements of $\wp X$ is the set of sets. \item for $X$ and $Y$, the set of elements of $\F {X}{Y}$ is the set of functions from elements of $X$ to elements of $Y$. \end{itemize}

Definition. Let $X$ and $T$ be sets. Then $T$ is closed under finite intersection, if for all elements $A$ and $B$ of $\wp X$, if $A \in T$ and $B \in T$, then $A \cap B \in T$.

Definition. Let $X$ and $T$ be sets. Then $T$ is closed under arbitrary union, if for all elements $I$ of $\wp \mathbb{ U}$, for all elements $U$ of $\mathrm{Family}$ applied to $X$ and $I$, if for all elements $i$ of $I$, $\elemFam {U}{i}\in T$, then $\famCup {I}{U}\in T$.

Definition. Let $X$ and $T$ be sets. Then $T$ is a topology, if $\emptyset \in T$, $X \in T$ and $T$ is closed under finite intersection and closed under arbitrary union.

Definition. Let $X$ and $T$ be sets. Then $\toposet {X}{T}$ is a set.

Definition. Let $x$ be a set. Then we can say that $x$ is a topological space.

By cases: \begin{itemize} \item for $X$ and $T$, the proposition that $\toposet {X}{T}$ is a topological space is the proposition that $T$ is a topology. \end{itemize}

Definition. Let $X$ and $T$ be sets. Assume that $\toposet {X}{T}$ is a topological space. Let $U$ be a set. Then $U$ is open, if $U \in T$.

Definition. Let $X$ and $T$ be sets. Assume that $\toposet {X}{T}$ is a topological space. Let $F$ be a set. Then $F$ is close, if $X \setminus F$ is open.

Definition. Let $X$ and $T$ be sets. Assume that $\toposet {X}{T}$ is a topological space. Then $p$ is a Hausdorff space, if for all elements $x$ and $y$ of $X$, if it is not the case that $\mathrm{Equ}$ holds for $X$, $x$ and $y$, then there exist elements $\mathrm{U\_x}$ and $\mathrm{U\_y}$ of $\wp X$, such that $\mathrm{U\_x} \in T$, $\mathrm{U\_y} \in T$, $x \in \mathrm{U\_x}$, $y \in \mathrm{U\_y}$ and $\mathrm{U\_x} \cap \mathrm{U\_y} = \emptyset$.

Definition. Let $X$ and $T$ be sets. Assume that $\toposet {X}{T}$ is a topological space. Let $N$ be an element of $\wp X$. Let $x$ be an element of $X$. Then $N$ is a neighborhood of $x$, if there exists an element $U$ of $\wp X$, such that $U \in T$ and $x \in U \subset N$.
